    id: "9a6db8fe8cf87dcfec83",
    cat: "conflitti",
    title: "Two killed near Israeli-occupied area in south Lebanon",
    summary: "Despite ceasefire reached between Israel and Lebanon in June, Israeli attacks continue.",
    preview: "Despite a ceasefire agreement reached between Israel and Lebanon in June, Israeli attacks continue. Two men have been killed in southern Lebanon’s Bint Jbeil district while taking part in an annual olive harvest, according to Lebanese state media and local authorities, as attacks by Israel continue. One of the men died Sunday in the village of Froun from a munition left behind by recent fighting and the other in Yater as a result of Israeli shelling that “targeted the outskirts of the town while he was inspecting his land and olive harvest”, according…",
    body: "Despite a ceasefire agreement reached between Israel and Lebanon in June, Israeli attacks continue.\n\nTwo men have been killed in southern Lebanon’s Bint Jbeil district while taking part in an annual olive harvest, according to Lebanese state media and local authorities, as attacks by Israel continue.\n\nOne of the men died Sunday in the village of Froun from a munition left behind by recent fighting and the other in Yater as a result of Israeli shelling that “targeted the outskirts of the town while he was inspecting his land and olive harvest”, according to the official National News Agency (NNA).\n\nRecommended Stories\n\nlist of 3 items\n\n- list 1 of 3Lebanese are watching Israel’s upcoming election, but few expect change\n\n- list 2 of 3Syria denies officials held ‘secret’ talks with Hezbollah in Turkiye\n\n- list 3 of 3‘Dire situation’: Lebanese prisoners on hunger strike for 18 days\n\nThe Lebanese army blamed Israeli tank fire and called the ongoing attacks “a flagrant breach of existing agreements”.\n\nAfterwards, the Yater municipality urged residents “to avoid the outskirts of the town and refrain from crossing the earthen barrier separating some unsafe neighbourhoods” for fear of Israeli attacks.\n\nIn Froun, the NNA reported that “a foreign object” left over from previous Israeli operations exploded and killed the man, who was an engineer, as he and others tended their trees.\n\nThe engineer had come down from Beirut, where he lives, for the harvest, Froun mayor Hassan Bazzi told AFP.\n\nThe incident prompted local residents to call off the harvest and wait for “the Lebanese army to complete the decontamination of the village”, Bazzi said.\n\nDisplaced people\n\nSince an agreement between Lebanon and Israel was struck in June, violence in the south has lessened, but not stopped.\n\nLebanon was pulled into conflict on March 2, when Hezbollah struck Israel in support of Tehran during the US-Israel war on Iran. Subsequently, Israel launched a bombing campaign and a ground invasion that authorities say have killed more than 4,300.\n\nIn August, Lebanon’s Prime Minister Nawaf Salam accused Israel of intentionally targeting homes and civilian infrastructure across the country. The International Organization for Migration (IOM) recorded nearly 350,000 internally displaced people in late August, a sign of the worsening displacement crisis.\n\nVolunteers from the nearby town of Jebchit and locals came to help their southern neighbours with the harvest in an act of solidarity.\n\n“From north to south, one people and one land,” said organiser Ahmad Chahhal.",
